# Single origin for development.
#
# Everything the browser touches comes from http://localhost:8000, which is
# how production works behind nginx. That removes the whole class of bugs
# where the API and the pages disagree about where the other one lives:
# no CORS preflight, no WP_API_BASE injected into the built HTML, and
# base_url in local.toml is one value rather than two.
#
# Caddy is used here rather than nginx for one concrete reason: mkdocs'
# live reload runs over a websocket, and reverse_proxy handles the Upgrade
# handshake by itself. In nginx you have to set proxy_set_header Upgrade
# and Connection manually, and forgetting kills live reload silently while
# everything else still looks fine.
#
# Listens on :80 inside the container network. The host port is decided
# entirely by the `ports:` mapping in compose.yml, so there is one place to
# change it rather than two that have to agree.
#
# The production config is a different file for a different job. See
# rest-api/nginx-api-snippet.conf, which is what actually goes on the VM.

{
	# No admin API and no automatic HTTPS: this only ever listens on
	# localhost and there is nothing to get a certificate for.
	admin off
	auto_https off
}

:80 {
	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}

	# Order matters: the API path has to be matched before the catch-all.
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy api:8080 {
			# The API reads this to record where a confirmation came
			# from, as proof of consent. Without it every subscriber
			# looks like it came from the proxy's container address.
			header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
		}
	}

	# The mailbox, so the whole stack needs one published port. Mailpit is
	# told its prefix with MP_WEBROOT, otherwise its own links point at /.
	handle /mailbox/* {
		reverse_proxy mailbox:8025
	}

	handle {
		# mkdocs serve, including its live reload websocket.
		reverse_proxy docs:8000
	}

	handle_errors {
		respond `waterpark dev proxy: {err.status_code} {err.status_text}

This response came from Caddy, not from the application. A 502 here means
the upstream container is not answering; check with:

  docker compose -f dev/compose.yml ps
  docker compose -f dev/compose.yml logs docs
  docker compose -f dev/compose.yml logs api

A 404 you see in the browser that does NOT carry this text came from
mkdocs or from the API, which means the proxy is working fine.
` {err.status_code}
	}
}
